WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.450
- Season 11, episode 21,
McDreamy says goodnight.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:05.283
Let's watch it together.

00:00:05.283 --> 00:00:06.719
Beep-wop.

00:00:06.719 --> 00:00:09.469
(engine revving)

00:00:11.793 --> 00:00:14.793
(vehicles slamming)

00:00:16.704 --> 00:00:19.621
(brakes squealing)

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:24.780
Okay, first mistake he's making here,

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.265
you need to call for help.

00:00:26.265 --> 00:00:27.323
(record scratching)

00:00:27.323 --> 00:00:29.090
(light, whimsical music)

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:29.990
- My phone's not working.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.350
- Because there's very limited things

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.637
you can do on the road without any tools.

00:00:33.637 --> 00:00:36.840
You you need to call for help
and get emergency transport

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:37.800
to get them to a hospital

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.420
where they could actually
be taken care of.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.010
- Hey, I can't open the door.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:41.843
- Winnie, Winnie.

00:00:41.843 --> 00:00:42.676
- Ma'am.

00:00:42.676 --> 00:00:43.509
- Winnie.

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:44.907
- Hey, hey, what's your name?

00:00:44.907 --> 00:00:45.740
- Sarah.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:46.610
- Sarah, who's Winnie?

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:47.443
- My daughter.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.923
- Hey, you're Winnie?

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.990
- I think I'm dead.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:55.310
- Well, you're not dead.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.020
I can feel a purse in your wrist,

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.330
and you can't be dead if
your heart's still beating.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.460
See, I'm a doctor and I'm telling you.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:02.600
You ever wanna know if you're dead?

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.750
Feel your pulse.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:04.880
- Maybe that's a little dark thing

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.450
to say to a child.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:07.283
- [Derek] Listen, I'm gonna get you out.

00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:08.140
It's gonna hurt a lot.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.700
- I don't know if it's
shock, or if it's that

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.440
my leg hurts so badly that
I almost can't recognize it.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:18.200
It almost feels normal, so
maybe it won't be so bad

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.210
getting me out.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.280
- Sounds like you dislocated your hip.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.053
- How does he know that?

00:01:22.053 --> 00:01:24.980
He's just feeling around
over there and he's like,

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.484
yeah, dislocated the hip.

00:01:26.484 --> 00:01:29.020
Yeah, no special tests, eh.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.580
- I'm gonna get you out of this car.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.240
- Okay.
- Down on the ground,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.830
and then pop it back into the socket.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:35.740
- How does he know it's dislocated

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.070
versus like a full-on
fracture that's displaced?

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:43.029
He just has miraculous
hands that have x-ray field.

00:01:43.029 --> 00:01:44.160
(Sarah screams)

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.890
- The dislocation has cut off
the blood supply to your leg.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.430
If I don't do something,
you could lose it.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:50.850
- So that's the only time
you would do a reduction

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.120
in the field like this.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.940
If you see that the vascular
supply, meaning the blood flow

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.650
that is going to the
lower limb is obstructed,

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.820
because of the dislocation.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:00.680
That can happen.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.020
In this case, yeah, he
should try and put it back

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.440
so that he restores blood flow.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.290
Otherwise, literally the rest of her leg

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.234
starts dying in necrosing.

00:02:08.234 --> 00:02:10.170
(bone cracks)
(Sarah yells)

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.660
Whenever you do a reduction
of dislocation, initially,

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.373
worst pain ever afterwards,
it feels kind of good.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:19.843
- Help, help.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.974
- Does it hurt anywhere?

00:02:23.974 --> 00:02:24.807
- No.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.420
I'm not supposed to be here,

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:28.253
I have to go.

00:02:28.253 --> 00:02:30.170
- Listen to me, you're probably in shock.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:31.024
Okay?

00:02:31.024 --> 00:02:32.300
You got a lot of adrenaline
going to your head right now.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.213
There might be something wrong with you

00:02:33.213 --> 00:02:34.700
that you don't feel right now.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.560
So I need you to slow down.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.600
- That is very accurate.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.020
In fact, paramedics are
trained to tell folks

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.640
who've been in car accidents to calm down

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.593
because it's very easy to
say, I'm fine, I'm fine.

00:02:45.593 --> 00:02:47.720
I don't need to go get emergency care

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.160
because you are on adrenaline.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.640
You don't feel your internal injuries

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.000
and you could potentially be bleeding out.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.640
And it's also so important to know

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.280
the mechanism of the injury.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.810
Like the fact that this was
such a high speed car accident

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.860
already puts her at a
risk of severe fractures.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.233
Vertebral fractures, internal bleeding.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.600
- I just need to undo a button

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:06.550
so I can look at your stomach.

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.810
- He's going to see it like bruising

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:13.810
or he's going to see a giant
laceration with intestines.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.280
- Got a hand it to you, you got it,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.513
go home and have yourself
a drink, you earned it.

00:03:21.807 --> 00:03:23.140
(soft music)

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.807
(phone buzzing)

00:03:29.389 --> 00:03:32.370
- Oh, and that's why you don't stop

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:33.670
in the middle of the road.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.070
- Blunt force trauma to the head,

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:38.360
chest and abdomen, persistent hypotension

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.120
after two liters of saline.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:41.430
Pulse is thready at 130.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:42.960
- Okay so that's important information.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.720
So after giving him two liters of fluid

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.350
to try and bump up his blood pressure,

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.950
it's still persistently low,

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:49.480
which means that he's in a dangerous spot.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.030
Ideally they want to give him more fluids,

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.590
more blood products if that fails,

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:53.890
you have to start them on pressors

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.600
in order to keep his blood pressure up,

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.910
to give sufficient blood
flow to the vital organs.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:03:58.996
Also, they sent his blood.

00:03:58.996 --> 00:04:02.750
His pulse is 130, which means
that it's fast tachycardic.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.050
Reason being is when the
blood pressure's low.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.100
The heart tries to
compensate by beating faster

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.800
in order to like fake
increase the pressure.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:10.800
- [Doctor] Can you hear me?

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:11.840
- Yes, yes.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:12.693
I can hear you.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.510
Loss of verbal skills,

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:16.343
possible bleeding in the brain.

00:04:16.343 --> 00:04:18.240
- [Doctor] His pupils
are equal and reactive.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.150
- Good sign.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:19.983
Still should get a CT.

00:04:19.983 --> 00:04:21.460
- He's got a flail chest in the right.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.210
I need a 36 french tube now.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:24.810
- Alright so they said
he has a flail chest.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:27.050
That means he has
significant rib fractures,

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:28.810
like complete fractures on one side,

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.400
maybe even a pneumothorax,

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.060
because what happens is when
you have a complete disconnect

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.370
of your chest like that,

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:38.110
your breathing happens
paradoxically on the side

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.240
where you have your injury.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.173
- This is gonna to hurt,
but we have to do it.

00:04:45.145 --> 00:04:48.612
(incision sounds)

00:04:48.612 --> 00:04:51.090
(deep breathe)

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.010
- They're basically putting
in a chest tube to drain it.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:56.610
His chest of blood because
he's bleeding internally,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.940
which will allow his lungs
and potentially his heart

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.500
to expand when they are
beating and filling up

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.210
with either air or blood respectively.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.530
You also, sometimes if
you have a pneumothorax,

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.930
a collapsed lung, when
you equalize the pressure,

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:10.040
you allow the lung to expand as well.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:11.380
- He fixed our ride.

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:13.200
He helped Charlie.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.150
And then he put all of us
inside his back and he saved us.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:17.210
- He saved you?

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:18.840
- All of us.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:19.983
He's a doctor.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:22.557
- He's a doctor.

00:05:22.557 --> 00:05:23.980
- So you should treat him differently

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:26.630
than you would any other patient what.

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.473
- We've got you, and
it's all gonna be okay.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:31.963
- This isn't right.

00:05:33.442 --> 00:05:36.310
You should have taken me to get a head CT.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.910
- Anytime you have blunt
force trauma to the head

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.850
with a serious accident
again, mechanism of injury.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:44.030
Semi-truck hitting you
at a fast rate of speed.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:45.330
Get CT's.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:48.870
We over-order CT's in
emergency rooms for things

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.760
that don't really matter yet when people

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.830
have a really high-speed car accident,

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:54.150
people are like should we get a head CT?

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.480
Yes. You need to figure out if
there's bleeding internally.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.740
Because you're management
completely changes.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:05:59.980
- We have to work fast people.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:02.620
- I'm stable guys, I'm stable.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.260
Take me to get the head CT.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.600
- The way you decide if someone's stable

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:06.560
is you look at their vitals.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.660
If his blood pressure's not tanking,

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:10.330
you know that the patient is stable enough

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:11.830
to go get a head CT.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.080
And if there's bleeding in the brain,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.720
you want to save the brain
because without the brain,

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:16.000
there is no body.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.210
- He's got a great tooth blown apart.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:18.270
- Meredith would leave it.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:19.120
- Let's leave it.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:20.930
- At the four quadrants.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:23.270
- And she'd double-check
the retrohepatic space.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.490
- The retrohepatic space is
a very commonly known one

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.030
that you learned in
your surgery rotations,

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:31.876
where you can lose a lot of
blood and not even know it.

00:06:31.876 --> 00:06:32.714
- He is still hypertensive.

00:06:32.714 --> 00:06:33.547
- Heart is intact.

00:06:33.547 --> 00:06:34.540
He's not bleeding in his chest,

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:35.373
this makes no sense.

00:06:35.373 --> 00:06:36.206
- I'm missing something.

00:06:36.206 --> 00:06:37.370
- Start on TEE.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:38.203
- Not that.

00:06:38.203 --> 00:06:39.110
That's not what you're missing.

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:42.330
- A TEE is transesophageal echocardiogram

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.980
where essentially you
put an ultrasound probe

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:47.880
into the esophagus in order
to better visualize the heart.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.493
- You got a headlock check.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:52.540
Check his pupils.

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.190
- His right pupil is blown.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:59.660
- Anisocoria can happen
when you are having bleeding

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:00.493
into the brain.

00:07:00.493 --> 00:07:02.560
- Pave to neurosurgery,
right freaking now.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.810
- The hell have you people screwed up now.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:09.650
- Us, you're on call for trauma.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.404
What took you so long?

00:07:11.404 --> 00:07:12.237
- Who the hell are you?

00:07:12.237 --> 00:07:13.920
- Your response to him was
supposed to be 20 minutes.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:15.300
- It wouldn't have made a difference.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:16.700
- If you guys had ordered a head CT,

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:17.980
you would call me sooner.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:20.120
- You were supposed to
be here in 20 minutes,

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:21.620
not an hour and a half.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:24.190
We have a chance. He
had a freaking chance.

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.380
Get him an IOR.

00:07:26.422 --> 00:07:27.255
- What?

00:07:27.255 --> 00:07:29.570
- That type of blame game can
happen in a hospital setting

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:31.290
where there's big egos.

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.370
I mean, obviously both are in the wrong.

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:34.637
Does it matter who's more wrong?

00:07:34.637 --> 00:07:35.470
No.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:36.610
The only thing that should
matter is the patient

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:39.280
and try to do the best
that they could now.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:40.163
- It's too late.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.180
- The call to not get a
head CT was a bad call.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:49.300
- We're not a trauma
center or a teaching--

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:50.570
- You did the best you could.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:52.140
- I mean a head CT.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:54.830
Like if they made a mistake
with some kind of protocol,

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:56.430
I get it because they're
not a trauma center.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:57.263
That makes sense.

00:07:57.263 --> 00:08:00.020
But not getting a head CT
is just incredible to me.

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:03.090
Like that's the first thing
you would do in a car accident

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:03.923
of that nature.

00:08:03.923 --> 00:08:05.340
Remember the mechanism of injury there,

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:09.220
in fact, there's like a
Canadian head CT protocol.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:12.420
And one of the points is
the mechanism of injury.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:14.550
And it's like, if it's a
high speed accident, boom,

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:15.660
you get the head CT.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.520
Like I still even remember
that from med school.

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.810
I also don't quite understand
how they were certain

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:22.010
that he had abdominal bleeding over,

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:23.450
like how do they know he wasn't bleeding

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:24.910
into his brain versus his abdomen?

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:26.690
Like it seemed like they just guessed that

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:27.810
because if you're doing anything,

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:28.990
your pants scanning the patient,

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:29.823
you're scanning the head.

00:08:29.823 --> 00:08:30.920
You're scanning the lungs.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:32.410
I mean the chest and the abdomen,

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:33.690
you're doing it all at once.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:34.523
- Mrs. Shepherd.

00:08:34.523 --> 00:08:36.260
There's some things you need to know.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:37.820
Some things we need to discuss.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.240
Difficult things.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:40.280
- I'm a doctor.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.470
You've waited the
requisite number of hours

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:45.390
and now you can officially
declare him dead.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.470
So yes, you needs a bed.

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:48.670
And those must be the papers.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:51.540
- Essentially, the ICU wants
to clear the patient out

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.500
if there's no improvements being made.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:56.900
And while that is part of what goes

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:58.740
into medical decision-making obviously

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:00.130
when it comes to triage,

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:02.240
but also it's in the
patient's best interest

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.930
to not be further tortured

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.860
if there is no potential
chance of recovery.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:09.430
- I'd stop all curative intervention.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.670
Discontinue all routine monitoring,

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.610
remove all the catheters,
drains, and tubes

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:17.720
and any and all treatments
that might provide comfort

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:19.040
to the patient.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.230
Terminate all life-sustaining measures.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:25.070
- Well see that's not exactly true.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.740
When we fill out a POLST form,

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:29.000
we actually can put orders in

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:31.960
that would put a patient on comfort care.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:33.970
So we would put orders in to make sure

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:35.420
the patient is comfortable.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:36.740
Giving them high sedation,

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:39.410
giving them pain control, mucus control,

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:41.360
cough control, hiccup control.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:43.750
There's all sorts of things
that we need to think about

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:46.110
for a patient who's at
the end of their lives.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:48.300
And we do that with our
comfort care order set.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.810
When we choose to say that
aggressively treating someone

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:54.110
causes them more harm than actual benefit.

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:55.550
We're not saying discontinue everything.

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:57.300
We're discontinuing certain things

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:58.820
that we feel like harm the patient.

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:00.870
And we continue certain
things or begin things

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:02.370
that we think would benefit the patient,

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:04.160
meaning that the goal is no longer

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:05.740
to make the patient live longer,

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:08.213
but to make the patient as
comfortable as possible.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:12.973
Oh no McDreamy is going to die.

00:10:12.973 --> 00:10:15.556
(somber music)

00:10:21.201 --> 00:10:23.050
One of the mistakes
I've seen residents make

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.290
is when they are putting
a patient on comfort care

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.870
and they're discontinuing the ventilator

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:27.790
and all these things.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:29.520
They forget to turn off the alarms

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.300
because remember the
alarms just notify you

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:33.520
when a patient's crashing
and it's very discomforting

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.440
for a family to watch their
loved one go peacefully.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.270
But then the alarms going off like crazy

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:39.270
that the patient's dying.

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:40.855
So you've got to turn those off as well.

00:10:40.855 --> 00:10:43.522
(alarm beeping)

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.153
And they didn't turn off the alarms.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:52.880
McDreamy.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:55.180
One of the hardest
conversations in medicine

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:56.890
is the end of life conversation.

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:58.020
And this is supposed to happen,

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:00.350
not just with someone
who's terminally ill,

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:01.980
someone who's just been in a car accident.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.710
It's supposed to happen with
your primary care doctor.

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:07.300
So you can have a plan
because without a plan,

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:09.340
so many things can go wrong.

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:10.173
What's to happen?

00:11:10.173 --> 00:11:11.006
What are their wishes?

00:11:11.006 --> 00:11:12.570
Who's going to be the power of attorney?

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:15.510
What do they want from their
care, medically speaking.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.400
And an important aspect of
that is understanding that

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:20.670
there's never a sure-fire correct answer

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:23.300
because patients a lot of times
will ask of me as a doctor.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:24.133
Should I do this?

00:11:24.133 --> 00:11:25.630
Should I take them off life support?

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:27.120
Do they have a chance to get better?

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.200
A lot of times you have to
give the most accurate answer,

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:32.330
but it's not a hundred
percent accurate answer.

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:34.670
A lot of folks are not
comfortable with that.

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:37.540
But the unfortunate
reality is that's life.

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:40.280
Every decision cannot
be a hundred percent.

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:41.910
And ultimately it's your choice

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:43.450
that has to be decided upon.

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:45.870
Based on the wishes of
what the patient wanted.

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:49.370
Here's the difference between
real doctors and TV doctors,

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:50.520
click here to check that out.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:53.030
Great video I share some
epic patient stories

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:55.480
and as always, stay happy and healthy.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:56.453
Unlike McDreamy.

00:11:57.874 --> 00:12:00.457
(upbeat music)

